{
  "source": "Prompts/ai-is-math-v7.mp4",
  "duration": 206.144,
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 5.82,
      "text": "When you type a prompt into chat GPT and receive a coherent response, it can feel like the"
    },
    {
      "start": 5.82,
      "end": 11.46,
      "text": "system is thinking. Tools like Claude appear to understand context and nuance, leading"
    },
    {
      "start": 11.46,
      "end": 17.36,
      "text": "us to wonder what is actually happening behind the interface. These systems function by calculating"
    },
    {
      "start": 17.36,
      "end": 23.2,
      "text": "probability, analyzing the statistical likelihood of what should happen next. This process"
    },
    {
      "start": 23.2,
      "end": 29.52,
      "text": "of predicting outcomes based on specific data traces back to 1654, when mathematicians"
    },
    {
      "start": 29.52,
      "end": 35.28,
      "text": "Blaise Pascal and Pierre de Fermat exchanged letters about the odds in gambling. Their work"
    },
    {
      "start": 35.28,
      "end": 40.18,
      "text": "provided the foundation for how we calculate the chance of a future event, a framework that"
    },
    {
      "start": 40.18,
      "end": 46.66,
      "text": "remains central to how modern AI builds an answer. To see how that works, we start with"
    },
    {
      "start": 46.66,
      "end": 51.94,
      "text": "standard probability. This applies to scenarios where every possible outcome is equally likely."
    },
    {
      "start": 52.46,
      "end": 56.78,
      "text": "The math is a simple division. You count the ways to get the specific result you want,"
    },
    {
      "start": 56.78,
      "end": 63.42,
      "text": "and divide that by the total number of possible outcomes. Consider a basic scenario. You toss"
    },
    {
      "start": 63.42,
      "end": 70.4,
      "text": "two coins. To find the chance both land on heads, we map out the possibilities. There are four equally"
    },
    {
      "start": 70.4,
      "end": 77.14,
      "text": "likely results. You could get two heads, a head and a tail, a tail and a head, or two tails."
    },
    {
      "start": 77.72,
      "end": 83.18,
      "text": "Only one matches our goal. We divide that successful path by the four total outcomes,"
    },
    {
      "start": 83.18,
      "end": 89.84,
      "text": "giving a probability of 25%. At this stage, without any other information to go on, your"
    },
    {
      "start": 89.84,
      "end": 97.42,
      "text": "odds of seeing two heads are exactly one out of four. However, odds are rarely static. In"
    },
    {
      "start": 97.42,
      "end": 103.4,
      "text": "mathematics, conditional probability describes how new evidence changes the calculation. Imagine"
    },
    {
      "start": 103.4,
      "end": 108.82,
      "text": "we run the toss again, but this time, someone peaks. They tell you the first coin landed on"
    },
    {
      "start": 108.82,
      "end": 114.92,
      "text": "heads. Knowing the first coin is heads changes the landscape. Looking back at our original four"
    },
    {
      "start": 114.92,
      "end": 120.5,
      "text": "combinations, some are no longer possible. Because the first coin is heads, we can"
    },
    {
      "start": 120.5,
      "end": 125.78,
      "text": "immediately eliminate any outcome starting with tails. Those possibilities are ruled out."
    },
    {
      "start": 126.34,
      "end": 131.84,
      "text": "Our total pool of outcomes has shrunk from four to two. Out of those two remaining paths,"
    },
    {
      "start": 132.26,
      "end": 138.3,
      "text": "only one results in double heads. One divided by two means the probability jumps to 50%."
    },
    {
      "start": 138.3,
      "end": 143.92,
      "text": "The physical state of the coins didn't change, but your knowledge of them did. That new"
    },
    {
      "start": 143.92,
      "end": 150.84,
      "text": "information reshaped the odds from 25% to 50%. They use the sequence of a conversation to"
    },
    {
      "start": 150.84,
      "end": 156.76,
      "text": "constantly update the probability of what comes next. Every word you type in a prompt serves as"
    },
    {
      "start": 156.76,
      "end": 161.5,
      "text": "the evidence that narrows down the possibilities, just like knowing the result of the first coin"
    },
    {
      "start": 161.5,
      "end": 168.2,
      "text": "toss. If you ask, what should I name my new dog, and the AI begins its response with,"
    },
    {
      "start": 168.64,
      "end": 174.34,
      "text": "you could name him, those words become the context for the next calculation. Based on the words"
    },
    {
      "start": 174.34,
      "end": 180.16,
      "text": "already written, the AI calculates the likelihood for the next word in the sequence. It might"
    },
    {
      "start": 180.16,
      "end": 188.84,
      "text": "find a 22% chance for spot, 17% for max, and 14% for buddy. Once a word is chosen,"
    },
    {
      "start": 188.84,
      "end": 194.08,
      "text": "it is added to the text. This updated string of words becomes the new evidence for the next"
    },
    {
      "start": 194.08,
      "end": 201.52,
      "text": "calculation, and the cycle repeats instantly. AI builds answers with probabilities, one prediction"
    },
    {
      "start": 201.52,
      "end": 202.32,
      "text": "at a time."
    }
  ],
